@node Registering Opponents, Hooks, Insinuating Heroes, Insinuating Heroes
@subsection Registering Opponents

@cindex opponents, defined
@cindex AI
@cindex computer-controlled players

An @dfn{opponent} is a behavior that can be associated to a
computer-controlled player.  (You may prefer the term @dfn{AI}.)

If your plug-in implements an opponent, you must register it to Heroes
using this interface.

Practically, you should supply a set of functions that will be called to
drive some computer-controlled players.  Keep this plural in mind when
writing an opponent: a single opponent can be used to control several
vehicles.

@deftypefun void opponent_register (an_opponent_sig *@var{opp})
@deftypefunx void opponent_unregister (an_opponent_sig *@var{opp})

Register or unregister an opponents.  These functions are usually called
from the @code{initialize} and @code{finalize} functions of a plug-in.

It's possible for a module to register many opponents (e.g., for
different kinds of games), in which case the above functions must be
called several time.

@var{opp} is a pointer to an opponent signature, which is a structure
describing the opponent.  See below.

@end deftypefun

@subsubheading @code{an_opponent_sig}

@tindex an_opponent_sig
@findex initialize_player
@findex finalize_player
@findex start_player
@findex stop_player
@findex square_update
@findex frame_update

Here is the definition of the @code{an_opponent_sig} structure.

@example
typedef struct an_opponent_sig an_opponent_sig;
struct an_opponent_sig @{
  const char *name;
  an_opponent_type type;
  void *(*initialize_player)(const a_level_state *state, int player);
  void (*finalize_player)(const a_level_state *state, int player,
  void *(*start_player)(const a_level_state *state, int player,
                        void *callback_data);
  void *(*stop_player)(const a_level_state *state, int player,
                       void *callback_data);
  void (*square_update)(const a_level_state *state, int player,
                        an_opponent_action *action,
  void (*frame_update)(const a_level_state *state, int player,
                       an_opponent_action *action,
                       void *callback_data);
@};
@end example

You have to instantate a variable of this type for each opponent that
your plug-in registers.  Here is how the standard AI plug-in declares
its opponent for Quest and Death Match mode:

 @example
static an_opponent_sig ai_standard_deathm = @{
  "standard AI for Death Match",
  OT_QUEST | OT_DEATHM,
  &ai_level_initialize,
  &ai_level_finalize,
  0,
  0,
  &ai_goto_target,
  &ai_throttle
@};
@end example

Here is the meaning of each field.

@table @code
@item name
This is a name for the opponent plug-in.  This is not yet used today,
except for debugging messages, bug it might be used later to present to
the user a list of plug-ins to chose from.

@item type
@vindex OT_NONE
@vindex OT_QUEST
@vindex OT_DEATHM
@vindex OT_KILLEM
@vindex OT_TCASH
@vindex OT_COLOR

A mask indicating which mode this opponent can be used for.
You can use one or more (just @i{OR} them) of the following
values.
@table @code
@item OT_NONE
cannot play
@item OT_QUEST
can play quest mode
@item OT_DEATHM
can play death match mode
@item OT_KILLEM
can play kill'em all mode
@item OT_TCASH
can play time-ca$h mode
@item OT_COLOR
can play color mode
@end table

The @code{OT} prefix is a short for Opponent Type.

@item initialize_player
This is a function called after the game state have been initialized, so
that the opponent can prepare itself to play.  For example this is the
right place to allocate and/or precompute maps that your opponent needs
during the game.

This function is called once for each player that will be driven by the
current opponent.

The returned @code{void *} data will be passed back to the opponent in
following function calls.  You can use this to store per-player states.

@item finalize_player
This function is called after the level is over, so the opponent can do
some cleanup.  If you @code{initialize_player()} function allocated some
memory this is where it should be @code{free}d.  Also, don't forget to
@code{free} @var{callback_data} if you @code{malloc}ed it.

@item start_player
Unless @code{NULL}, this function is called every time the player is
introduced into the game (at the beginning of the new game, or after a
death).

The returned @code{void *} data will replace any previous
@var{callback_data} returned by @code{initialize_player()}
@code{start_player()}.  This is seldom needed, usually you will just
return @var{callback_data} as-is.

@item stop_player
Unless @code{NULL}, this function is called every time the player is
removed from the game (i.e., after a death).

As with @code{start_player()}, the returned @code{void *} will replace
@var{callback_data} in following calls to the opponent.

@item square_update
Unless @code{NULL}, this function is called each time the player enter a
new square.  This is usually where the opponent should decide what's the
next direction the player should take.  This must be done by changing
@var{action} (see the @code{a_throttle} description below).

@item frame_update
Unless @code{NULL}, this function is called on each frame (i.e., 70
times per second).  This is called frequently, so please, whatever you
do here, keep it fast!  As with @code{square_update()}, driving the player
can be done by fiddling with @var{action} (see the @code{a_throttle}
description below).

@end table

@subsubheading @code{a_throttle}

@tindex an_opponent_action
@tindex a_throttle

This structure is used by @code{square_update()} and
@code{frame_update()} to drive players.  It is very
simple.

@example
typedef struct an_opponent_action an_opponent_action;
struct an_opponent_action @{
  a_dir dir;
  a_throttle throttle;
@};
@end example

@table @code
@item dir
This is the current direction of the player.  The acceptable values are
@code{D_UP}, @code{D_RIGHT}, @code{D_DOWN}, and @code{D_LEFT}.

Changing this field will change the player's direction, but this can
only have an effect when the player enter a new square.  Hence if you
change @code{dir} from @code{square_update()} this will take effect
immediatly, but if you do that from @code{frame_update()} it will take
effect the next time a square is entered.

Usually the value set in @code{dir}, i.e., the direction of the player,
will not be change between calls to @code{square_update()} or
@code{frame_update()}.  However it can change if the player enter a
tunnel, hit a wall, or restart to play (after a die).

Finally, it may be worth to note that this is the @emph{real} direction
the player will take, even if the player has taken an @emph{inversed
commands} bonus.  In fact inversed commands apply only to humans.

@item throttle
@vindex TH_BRAKE
@vindex TH_NORMAL
@vindex TH_SPEEDUP
This is the accelerator pedal.  You can choose between three positions:
@code{TH_BRAKE}, @code{TH_NORMAL}, and @code{TH_SPEEDUP}.

Remember this is sticky: if you have set @code{throttle} to
@code{TH_BRAKE} or @code{TH_SPEEDUP} it's your job to set it back to
@code{TH_NORMAL} latter.

This will be reset to @code{TH_NORMAL} each time the player restart to
play.
@end table

