@node Hooks,  , Registering Opponents, Insinuating Heroes
@subsection Hooks

@cindex hooks, defined

@dfn{Hooks} are named places in the code, where you can attach your own
code (i.e. your own call-back functions).

Usually, you will want to attach some functions to existing hooks in
Heroes.  This API also allows you to create new hooks (this could be
used for inter-module communications, for instance).

The hook system implementation of Heroes is heavily inspired from the
one from Zsh (the Z shell).

@menu
* Hooking functions::
* Defining and running a hook::
@end menu

@node Hooking functions, Defining and running a hook, Hooks, Hooks
@subsubheading Hooking functions

@cindex Hooking functions
@cindex registering hook functions
@cindex unregistering hook functions

Given the name of a hook, you can subscribe (i.e. add a function) to and
unsubscribe from to it using the @code{hook_add_fun} and
@code{hook_rem_fun} functions.  The hooked functions should have the
following form:

@findex sample_hook_fun
@example
void
sample_hook_fun (const a_hook *@var{hook},
                 void *@var{hook_data},
                 void *@var{callback_data})
@{
  ...
@}
@end example

@tindex a_hook_fun
This is declared as the @code{a_hook_fun} type:
@example
typedef void (*a_hook_fun)(const a_hook *@var{hook},
                           void *@var{hook_data},
                           void *@var{callback_data});
@end example

The @var{hook} pointer points to the hook for which is function is being
called back.  This is almost useless, except to get the name of this
hook (@code{hook->name}).

@var{hook_data} might point to additional data provided by the hook
provider, its interpretation is hook-dependant (see the documentation
for the hook you use).

@var{callback_data} is a pointer that was supplied when the function was
added registered for the hook being run.

@deftypefun void hook_add_fun (const char *@var{name}, a_hook_fun @var{fun}, void *@var{callback_data})

Add @var{fun} to the hook named @var{name}.  When the hook is run, the
function @var{fun} will be called back and passed @var{callback_data}.

@end deftypefun

@deftypefun void* hook_rem_fun (const char *@var{name}, a_hook_fun @var{fun})

Remove @emph{one} occurence of @var{fun} from the hook named @var{name}.

If you have registered @var{fun} several times with different
@var{callback_data} and want to remove only one of them, you should use
the @code{hook_rem_fun_if} function defined below.

The returned value is the @var{callback_data} passed to
@code{hook_add_fun} when @var{fun} was registered.

@end deftypefun

@deftypefun void* hook_rem_fun_if (const char *@var{name}, a_hook_fun @var{fun}, void *@var{ref_data}, a_hook_rem_if_fun @var{if_fun})

Remove an occurence of @var{fun} from the list of functions registered
for the hook named @var{name}.  The occurence removed is the first
occurence for which @var{if_fun} returns true.

@code{a_hook_rem_if_fun} is a functional type defined as follows:

@tindex a_hook_rem_if_fun
@example
typedef bool (*a_hook_rem_if_fun)(void *@var{ref_data},
                                  void *@var{callback_data});
@end example

This is usually used to remove a function whose @var{callback_data} (as
indicated to @code{hook_add_fun}) matches something indicated by
@var{ref_data} (passed to @code{hook_rem_fun_if}).

The returned value is the @var{callback_data} passed to
@code{hook_add_fun} when the matched function was registered.

@end deftypefun


@node Defining and running a hook,  , Hooking functions, Hooks
@subsubheading Definining and running a hook

@cindex defining hooks
@cindex hooks, defining
@cindex running hooks
@cindex hooks, running
@tindex a_hook
@findex HOOK_DEF

The type @code{a_hook} is a struct whose definition should be considered
as internal detail.  To define a hook, you need to create a global
@code{a_hook} variable using the @code{HOOK_DEF} macro as follows:

@example
static a_hook sample_hook = HOOK_DEF ("sample");
@end example

@noindent
and then you should register the hook with

@example
hook_define (&sample_hook);
@end example

@code{sample_hook} is the name of your hook variable, whose address is
passed to @code{hook_define} or @code{hook_run}.  @code{"sample"} is the
symbolic name if you hook; this is the name that hook clients should
pass to @code{hook_add_fun} or @code{hook_rem_fun}.

@deftypefun void hook_define (a_hook *@var{hook})
@deftypefunx void hook_undefine (a_hook *@var{hook})

@code{hook_define} registers a hook, so it can be latter populated with
@code{hook_add_fun} and siblings.  @code{hook_undefine} does the
converse, obviously.

@end deftypefun

@deftypefun void hook_define_many (a_hook *@var{hook}, int @var{size})
@deftypefunx void hook_undefine_many (a_hook *@var{hook}, int @var{size})

Same as @code{hook_define} and @code{hook_undefine}, but @var{hook} is
an array of @var{size} hooks.

@end deftypefun

@deftypefun void hook_run (a_hook *@var{hook}, void *@var{hook_data})

Run all function added to @var{hook}, and pass them @var{hook_data}.

Note there is no garanty on the order into which registered functions
are called.

@end deftypefun

